{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>Model Tubes in Fusion 360 Structural Member</h2>
          <p>
            When you drop a STEP file, your tubes are scanned automatically for cross-section family (<code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect</code>, <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Round</code>, <code class="mono">Profile_Angle</code>, <code class="mono">Profile_Channel</code>), outer dimensions, and wall thickness.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-callout warning" style="margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
            <span class="doc-callout-icon">⚠️</span>
            <div>
              <strong>Use Autodesk Fusion 360 + Structural Member:</strong>
              Every CAD program writes slightly different surface math. Right now, automatic tube scanning is calibrated for <strong>Autodesk Fusion 360</strong> assemblies built with the <strong>Structural Member (<code class="mono">SM</code>)</strong> tool. Follow these 8 steps so your parts size and nest cleanly on the first load.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">CAD Environment</span>
              <span class="value">Autodesk Fusion 360 + <code class="mono">SM</code> Add-In</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Bend Unfolding Prep</span>
              <span class="value"><code class="mono">Modify → Combine</code> Connected Tube Bodies</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Automatic Setup</span>
              <span class="value">Auto Profile &amp; Pack Grouping</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Open Autodesk Fusion 360 &amp; Verify the Structural Member Add-In</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Launch <strong>Autodesk Fusion 360</strong> and open a new design (such as <code class="mono">rotarycam-demo</code>):
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Check the Structural Member Icons:</strong> In <strong>DESIGN &rarr; SOLID &rarr; CREATE</strong>, verify the four blue <strong>Structural Member</strong> icons (<span class="ui-chip">SM</span> Structural Member, Miter, Notch, and Trim/Extend) are present.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Set Document Units:</strong> Under <strong>Document Settings</strong> in the left <strong>Browser</strong>, work in millimeters (<code class="mono">mm, g</code>) or inches. You can still pick either imperial or metric tube sizes inside the Structural Member library.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 1: Fusion 360 workspace with the Structural Member (SM) add-in icons in the SOLID toolbar</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-1-fusion.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-1-fusion.png" alt="Step 1: Autodesk Fusion 360 workspace showing the Structural Member SM icons in the CREATE toolbar" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Click <code class="mono">Create Sketch</code> &amp; Select a Reference Plane</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Pick a plane for your tube centerline sketch:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Click <span class="ui-chip">Create Sketch</span>:</strong> Click <strong>Create Sketch</strong> at the left end of <strong>SOLID &rarr; CREATE</strong>.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Pick an Origin Plane:</strong> Click <code class="mono">XY</code>, <code class="mono">XZ</code>, or <code class="mono">YZ</code> in the 3D viewport.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 2: Clicking Create Sketch and selecting a reference origin plane</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-2-sketch.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-2-sketch.png" alt="Step 2: Clicking Create Sketch and selecting the origin plane in Fusion 360" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Draw Your Tube Centerline Sketch &amp; Click <code class="mono">Finish Sketch</code></h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Draw the centerline wireframe for your frame or bent tube:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Draw Straight Runs &amp; Bend Arcs:</strong> Use <strong>Line</strong> (<code class="mono">L</code>) for straight tubes and <strong>Fillet</strong> (<code class="mono">F</code>) or tangent arcs for bend corners. Here, the left vertical line is a straight square HSS post, and the D-shaped loop with two corner radii is a bent tube meeting that post.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Click <span class="ui-chip emerald">✓ Finish Sketch</span>:</strong> Click <strong>Finish Sketch</strong> in the top-right toolbar (or Sketch Palette) to return to the 3D Solid workspace.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 3: Drawing the 2D centerline wireframe with straight lines and tangent bend arcs</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-3-draw.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-3-draw.png" alt="Step 3: Completed 2D centerline sketch in Fusion 360 with straight lines and tangent corner fillets" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 4
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 04</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Use the Structural Member Plugin to Populate 3D Tube Profiles</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Turn your centerline sketch into hollow HSS or round pipe solids and cope the joints:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Open Structural Member (4a):</strong> Click <strong>CREATE &rarr; Structural Member &rarr; Structural Member Command</strong> (or press the <span class="ui-chip">SM</span> ribbon icon). The same menu gives you <strong>Miter Command</strong>, <strong>Notch Command</strong>, and <strong>Trim Extend Command</strong> for coping joints.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Pick Lines &amp; Profile Size (4b):</strong> Click a sketch segment, then set your tube in <strong>STRUCTURAL MEMBER COMMAND</strong>. For this post: <strong>Standard:</strong> <code class="mono">ANSI_AISC</code>, <strong>Family:</strong> <code class="mono">HSS</code>, <strong>Size:</strong> <code class="mono">1-1/2 x 1-1/2</code> (<code class="mono">38.1 &times; 38.1 mm</code>), and <strong>Thickness:</strong> <code class="mono">1/8</code> (<code class="mono">3.17 mm</code>). Click <strong>OK</strong>.</li>
              <li><strong>3. Sweep &amp; Notch the Remaining Loop (4c):</strong> Repeat for the other segments using round pipe around the U-shaped loop (<code class="mono">SM0001:1</code> through <code class="mono">SM0007:1</code> in the Browser). Run <strong>Notch / Trim Extend</strong> so the round loop saddles cleanly onto the square post.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4a: Launching Structural Member Command (plus Miter, Notch &amp; Trim Extend) from the Create menu</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-4a-sm-menu.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-4a-sm-menu.png" alt="Step 4a: Selecting Structural Member Command from the Fusion 360 Create menu" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <figure class="doc-figure" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4b: Configuring ANSI_AISC HSS 1-1/2 × 1-1/2 × 1/8 wall on the vertical post segment</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-4b-sm-dialog.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-4b-sm-dialog.png" alt="Step 4b: Configuring ANSI_AISC HSS 1-1/2 x 1-1/2 x 1/8 in the Structural Member Command dialog" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <figure class="doc-figure" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4c: All 7 structural member segments (SM0001:1 through SM0007:1) generated and trimmed</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-4c-sm-complete.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-4c-sm-complete.png" alt="Step 4c: Completed 3D structural member assembly with SM0001:1 through SM0007:1 in the Fusion 360 Browser" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 5
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-5">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 05</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Combine Bent Tube Segments <em>(Only for Cut-and-Bend)</em></h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Structural Member creates a separate body for every line and arc in your sketch (<code class="mono">SM0002:1</code> through <code class="mono">SM0007:1</code>).
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Cutting straight separate tubes?</strong> Leave each structural member as its own body.</li>
              <li><strong>Unfolding a bent tube for Cut-and-Bend?</strong> Click <span class="ui-chip">Modify &rarr; Combine</span>. Pick the first bent segment as <strong>Target Body</strong>, then select all connected elbows and straight legs of that tube as <strong>Tool Bodies</strong>. Set <strong>Operation</strong> to <strong>Join</strong>, uncheck <em>New Component</em> and <em>Keep Tools</em>, and click <strong>OK</strong>.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-callout info">
              <span class="doc-callout-icon">💡</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Why Combine for Bending:</strong> Joining the straight legs and elbows into <strong>one solid body</strong> tells the bend unfolder that those pieces come from one continuous stick of stock with kerf-bending relief cuts (see <a href="bending.html" style="color: var(--cyan-arc);">Tutorial 04: Bending HSS &amp; HSS-O</a>). Do <em>not</em> combine separate tubes that weld together at a joint. Keep the vertical square post <code class="mono">SM0001:1</code> separate from the curved round loop.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 5: Using Modify → Combine (Join) to merge connected straight and bent segments into one solid body</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-5-combine.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-5-combine.png" alt="Step 5: Combining contiguous straight and curved structural member segments into a single solid body in Fusion 360" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 6
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-6">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 06</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Export a STEP File (<code class="mono">.step</code> / <code class="mono">.stp</code>) of the Entire Design</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Export the whole assembly in one STEP file:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Open File &rarr; Export:</strong> Click <strong>File</strong> in the top-left bar and choose <strong>Export...</strong></li>
              <li><strong>2. Pick STEP Format:</strong> Set <strong>Type</strong> to <span class="ui-chip">STEP Files (*.stp *.step)</span>.</li>
              <li><strong>3. Click <span class="ui-chip cyan">Export</span>:</strong> Save your <code class="mono">.step</code> file.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 6: Exporting the entire Fusion 360 assembly as a STEP file (*.stp *.step)</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-6-export.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-6-export.png" alt="Step 6: Exporting the entire Fusion 360 design as a STEP file" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 7
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-7">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 07</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Load the Exported STEP File into RotaryCAM</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Open RotaryCAM (<code class="mono">Pipe CAM</code>) and load your <code class="mono">rotarycam-demo.step</code> file:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Drop Your STEP File (7a):</strong> Drag <code class="mono">rotarycam-demo.step</code> into <strong>CAD INGESTION (Drop STEP or ZIP Bundle)</strong> in the left sidebar, or click to browse.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Click <span class="ui-chip cyan">Load &amp; Process CAD</span> (7b):</strong> Preview your 3D assembly in the viewport. Keep <code class="mono">☑ One-Shot</code> checked in the top bar, then click <strong>Load &amp; Process CAD</strong> to extract features, unfold bends, and build G-code automatically.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 7a: Dragging or selecting the exported .step file in the CAD Ingestion panel</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-7a-drop.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-7a-drop.png" alt="Step 7a: Dropping the exported STEP file into the RotaryCAM CAD Ingestion panel" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <figure class="doc-figure" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 7b: Previewing rotarycam-demo.step in 3D and clicking Load &amp; Process CAD</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-7b-load.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-7b-load.png" alt="Step 7b: 3D preview of rotarycam-demo.step and clicking Load & Process CAD" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 8
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-8">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 08</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Check Autodetected Tube Profiles &amp; Pack Groups</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Once processing finishes, the built-in tube scanner identifies every profile in your assembly with zero manual typing:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Check Cross-Section &amp; Dimensions (<span class="ui-chip">AI</span> Badge):</strong> Look at <strong>CROSS-SECTION &amp; DIMENSIONS</strong> in the left sidebar. You will see <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect</code>, outer dimensions <code class="mono">38.1&times;38.1 mm</code> (<code class="mono">1-1/2&Prime; &times; 1-1/2&Prime;</code>), wall thickness <code class="mono">3.17 mm</code> (<code class="mono">1/8&Prime;</code>), and stock length <code class="mono">110.0 mm</code>.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Check Pack Nesting Groups:</strong> Open the <strong>CUTTING PROFILE</strong> dropdown in the center bar. Both tube profiles (<code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect (38.1x38.1mm) (1 part)</code> and <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Round (OD 42.2mm) (1 part)</code>) are grouped separately by stock size, ready to simulate and export G-code.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 8: Autodetected Profile_HSS_Rect (38.1×38.1×3.17mm) and Profile_HSS_Round (OD 42.2mm)</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/ai-cad-8-autodetect.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/ai-cad-8-autodetect.png" alt="Step 8: RotaryCAM AI Cross-Section & Dimensions card and Multi-Part Pack Nesting dropdown showing autodetected rectangular and round tube profiles" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>
{% endblock %}
